module gunbc.language_target_registry


data language_target_registry_totality_law: String = "NO INVISIBLE REMAINDER (operator requirement C, 2026-08-04). This registry may not read as complete because two specimen rows exist. Every target-bearing surface this repository currently carries is EITHER joined to a registered subject OR appears here as an exact row with no identity, which the join kernel then reports as SurfaceMissingTargetIdentity. A surface that is simply absent from these lists is the one state the wall cannot see, which is why the population definition below is declared rather than implied, and why the TargetVocabulary rows carry an explicit scaffold note rather than a silent transcription. The registry now registers 26 subjects, so most rows below ARE identified; the ones that are not — machine_code and the legacy frontier prose table — each carry a note stating why joining them to a subject would be WRONG rather than merely undone, and both are deliberate. CORRECTED 2026-08-04 (review 48151): this sentence previously also listed the two runtime checkers as unidentified, which stopped being true in this same change when cargo was bound to Rust and the interpreter to Python. The authority describing the census contradicted the census two hundred lines below it — the stale-prose class this registry has now hit four times in a day, and the reason every correction here quotes what it replaced."

data language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note: String = "DECLARED SCAFFOLD, with its dissolution trigger — the TargetVocabulary rows below are HAND-DECLARED, and they were meant to be derived. The intent was to read v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment's target_ast_vocab_modules and target_ast_vocab_module_prefixes directly, so a module added to the lens would appear here automatically as an unidentified surface and the second-hardcoded-roster fork would close by construction. THAT IMPORT DOES NOT COMPILE TODAY, and the blocker is not in this module: the lens fails to resolve fold_list and list_snoc_item from v2.std.algebra even when compiled from its OWN source root as the entry — measured 2026-08-04. That is the Class B pool-membership defect DESIGN's import-strip open thread describes: a module's bare cross-module references resolve only when some unrelated import elsewhere in the assembled closure has already dragged the target into the pool, so the lens resolves inside the whole-corpus compile and not in a narrow closure. Transcribing the two lists here would be the workaround DESIGN section 5 forbids — an unmarked second representation of the very roster this registry exists to unfork — so they are declared as a counted scaffold instead. DISSOLVE-ON: the lens resolves under a narrow closure (Class B fix), at which point these rows become the fold over its lists; and ultimately the reverse direction, the lens deriving its confined set FROM this registry, which is the prerequisite edge from the support-registry obligation to the no-new-debt-gate obligation in the governing strategy contract, and is not done here. Those obligations are identified in a private strategy overlay rather than in this repository, so they are described by role and not named — publishing an overlay identifier is an explicit act under that repository's opt-in-public model, and it has not been authorised."

data language_target_registry_population_definition: String = "Target-bearing surfaces enumerated from six declared authorities: (1) StdLanguageCatalogue — std.languages Language rows; (2) TargetModelDeclaration — committed TargetModel-bearing language modules; (3) EmitHostExecution — tools.emit_host_transport per-language smoke fns; (4) TargetVocabulary — the confined module list and prefix list v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment enforces; (5) RuntimeChecker — external toolchains a support claim would execute against; (6) LegacyFrontier — gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier's prose rows. ALL SIX authorities are HAND-DECLARED at this revision — authority 4 was intended to be derived and is a counted scaffold with a stated trigger (language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note); nothing in this module is derived from the live tree. GRAIN, because it decides whether a row can be checked: a locus is either a real module path or a declared PREFIX over several (the extdeps.languages family paths cover multiple modules each, and v2.workflow.bash is a prefix over two, matching the containment lens's own grain). Distinguishing a legitimate prefix from a path that names nothing requires testing exact-match and prefix-match separately — the instrument fierce-heron-301 used to find three phantom rows among nine legitimate ones (2026-08-04). AND THE PREFIX MUST BE A STRING PREFIX, NOT A NAMESPACE ONE, which is not a quibble: a full sweep of this census on 2026-08-04 reported v2.workflow.bash as naming nothing when prefix was read as namespace containment, because the two modules under it are v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize and v2.workflow.bash_emit — siblings sharing a name stem, not children of a v2.workflow.bash namespace. The containment lens confines by string prefix, so the census must match that grain or it manufactures phantoms out of the lens's real rows. Under the corrected instrument the census has zero phantoms."

data language_target_registry_maximum_conclusion: String = "MAXIMUM CONCLUSION — what a green accounting or integrity check here does and does not establish. IT ESTABLISHES: every surface in the declared population above is accounted for, each either joined to a registered subject or reported as an exact unidentified row, and that no malformed join input is present; IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the declared population is COMPLETE with respect to the repository. All six authorities are hand-declared, so a target-bearing surface nobody wrote a row for is invisible to every assertion over this module — the failure mode loyal-ant-382 measured at 43 percent under-selection on a text-keyed census and this session reproduced at 100 percent on the ShellCommand sink. NOR DOES IT ESTABLISH THAT A DECLARED ROW NAMES A REAL SURFACE: three census rows were found on 2026-08-04 to be file paths transcribed as module paths, naming modules that do not exist, and no assertion in this module could have caught them because the population is the thing being asserted over. Closing both directions needs a Node-tree observer deriving the population from the real module tree, which is the residue-census obligation and is NOT built here. No count in this module may be quoted as a corpus claim."

data registry_revision_note: String = "WHY THERE IS NO PINNED REVISION STAMP HERE, removed rather than corrected (2026-08-04). An earlier revision of this module carried language_target_registry_subject_revision as a hand-written commit SHA, on the reasoning that a row set without its revision is a measurement pretending to be a standing fact. The reasoning was right and the mechanism was self-defeating in two ways. FIRST, IT WAS ALREADY FALSE: the stamp named the commit current when it was typed, and the row set then changed in four subsequent commits — including the one taking the registry from one target to three — so the declaration asserted an enumeration that had not held for some time. A review approved it as anchored honestly, which is the tell that a stale stamp reads as provenance rather than as the claim it actually is. SECOND, AND DECISIVELY, IT CANNOT BE FIXED BY UPDATING: pinning the SHA of the commit that contains the pin is self-referential, so any such stamp is stale by at least one commit BY CONSTRUCTION, and each update re-copies a number from the tree — the measure() == measure() collapse DESIGN section 5 names. The rows ARE anchored, by the thing that already anchors them: git records exactly which revision this file held which rows, at line grain, without anyone maintaining it. A second hand-kept representation of that fact is the section 3 duplication this registry exists to remove, applied to the registry itself."

data subject_ref_derivation_note: String = "EVERY SUBJECT REF BELOW IS A PROJECTION OF ITS SCOPE, never re-authored beside it. An earlier revision declared gnu_bash_subject_ref as a hand-written DeclarationRef repeating the module path and decl name the scope already carries — two writable representations of one identity, which can disagree exactly the way target_grounding_law describes. Reading scope.subject instead makes the disagreeing state unrepresentable: there is only one place the subject is stated, and it is the carrier that also holds the citations. Verified by execution 2026-08-04 that a top-level data declaration may project a field of an imported scope, so this costs nothing."

data gnu_bash_subject_ref: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef = gnu_bash_model_scope.subject.declaration

data d0_subject_registration_note: String = "D0 IS THE ROWS, AND THIS IS WHAT REPLACED THE BLOCKER NOTE THAT STOOD HERE. The previous revision recorded that registering TypeScript, C, C++, Verilog, SPICE, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, Swift, LLVM IR, WebAssembly, PTX, machine code, Lean, SQL, HTML, CSS, ECMAScript, JSON, YAML and Markdown was impossible because the construction wall requires a TargetGrounding and NONE of them had a cited subject declaration — Bash was the only one, and it had only just been authored. Twenty-three cited subject carriers now exist under dag/extdeps/languages/*/subject.dag, each enrolled in scope_carrier_paths, and the registry registers 26 targets: 24 external plus the two project-grounded ones. THE CITATION RULE THIS BATCH FOLLOWED, because it is the whole value of the rows: every locator was FETCHED LIVE on 2026-08-04 and its title and publisher confirmed from the response before it was written down. None was recalled. Where an upstream could not be read, the target was NOT registered and the cause was recorded in unregistered_target_findings — three of them — because a plausible URL for an unverified authority would make the wall green while grounding it in nothing, which is worse than the honest absence the blocker note described."

data unregistered_target_finding_note: String = "TYPED NON-REGISTRATIONS — the residue of verify-or-refuse, carried as rows so it is COUNTED and prioritizable rather than absorbed into silence (the extdeps.pricing.object_storage RateUnavailable precedent, where vendors whose rates were unreadable landed as typed unavailability instead of being filled from memory). Each row names a target that a reader would reasonably expect in the registry, and the exact reason it is absent. Two distinct causes appear and they need different work: an unreadable upstream authority is fixed by obtaining the document, while a fused surface is fixed by decomposing the surface — no amount of citation work registers x86-64 while machine_code still fuses three ISAs into one coproduct. These are NOT parked targets: a parked target is registered and grounded, these are ungrounded and therefore unwritable as registrations by construction."

type TargetNonRegistrationCause
  = UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable { attempted_locator: String, observed: String }
  | SurfaceFusesIndependentSubjects { surface_module: String, fused_subjects: String }

type UnregisteredTargetFinding {
  target_label: String
  cause: TargetNonRegistrationCause
}

data unregistered_target_findings: List<UnregisteredTargetFinding> = [
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "SQL (ISO/IEC 9075)",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "www.iso.org/standard/76583.html",
      observed: "HTTP 403 Forbidden — the ISO catalogue refuses this fetch, so neither the standard designation nor its title could be confirmed from the source. Note the contrast with C and C++, which ARE registered: their ISO standards are equally unreadable at iso.org, but their working groups WG14 and WG21 publish readable committee pages that state the designation, and SQL's SC32/WG3 has no equivalent readable page that was found. NEXT TRIGGER: any readable authority that states the ISO/IEC 9075 designation and title. Separately, this repository's SQL surfaces carry a SqlDialect coproduct over PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite, so even once the standard is cited, the dialects are independently governed implementations needing their own subjects."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "x86-64",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html",
      observed: "HTTP 403 Forbidden from Intel; the AMD64 Architecture Programmer's Manual at amd.com timed out after 60s. x86-64 is additionally DUAL-GOVERNED — Intel and AMD each publish their own architecture manuals for it — so registering it needs a decision about whether that is one subject with two citations or two subjects, which cannot be made without reading either document. NEXT TRIGGER: either vendor manual becomes readable."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "Aarch64",
    cause: UpstreamAuthorityUnreadable {
      attempted_locator: "developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0487/latest/",
      observed: "The Arm developer site redirects to support.arm.com and renders its documentation through JavaScript; the fetch returned only the page chrome ('Documentation - Arm Developer') with no title, document number, or architecture profile readable. The document number DDI 0487 is widely known, but writing it here from memory is exactly the recalled citation this batch refuses. NEXT TRIGGER: a readable Arm Architecture Reference Manual locator."
    }
  },
  UnregisteredTargetFinding {
    target_label: "machine code (the surface, not an ISA)",
    cause: SurfaceFusesIndependentSubjects {
      surface_module: "v2.extdeps.languages.machine_code",
      fused_subjects: "X86_64Isa, Aarch64Isa, RiscV64Isa — three independently governed upstreams in one Isa coproduct. See machine_code_fusion_note. RISC-V is separately registered as its own subject because its authority verified; this row records that the SURFACE still cannot join one identity, which is a decomposition obligation and not a citation one."
    }
  }
]

data legacy_frontier_label_fusion_note: String = "FILED, NOT FIXED (direction from the lane owner, 2026-08-04). These are findings about the PROSE self-host frontier table, whose rows the per-subject carriers are meant to replace. They are carried as typed rows rather than prose because the point of filing them is that they are COUNTABLE and joinable during that migration — a paragraph describing a fused label cannot be enumerated, and this table is exactly what someone migrating the rows will need to consult. WHY THEY MATTER MORE TOGETHER THAN SEPARATELY: the machine_code coproduct fuses three ISAs in the TYPE layer, and the frontier table independently fuses the same targets in its LABEL layer. One defect visible at two layers is stronger evidence than either sighting alone, because it shows the fusion was not an artifact of how one carrier happened to be written. The targets named here ARE registered and correctly grounded — nothing about their subjects is in doubt; what is defective is the legacy label that a migration would otherwise match them by."

type LegacyLabelFinding {
  legacy_label: String
  distinct_subjects_under_it: String
  consequence: String
}

data legacy_frontier_label_findings: List<LegacyLabelFinding> = [
  LegacyLabelFinding {
    legacy_label: "cpp — Phase-0-C / cpp — full C target",
    distinct_subjects_under_it: "c_language (ISO/IEC 9899) and cpp_language (ISO/IEC 14882)",
    consequence: "The frontier table carries two rows both labelled 'cpp' for what are two SEPARATELY STANDARDISED languages with different committees, WG14 and WG21. A migration matching rows to subjects by label would either merge C into C++ or have to guess from the row's prose which one 'Phase-0-C' meant. Both subjects are registered here with their own citations, so the fix at migration time is to match on the subject, never on this label."
  },
  LegacyLabelFinding {
    legacy_label: "machine_code / ptx",
    distinct_subjects_under_it: "ptx_language (NVIDIA PTX ISA) and riscv_isa (RISC-V International), plus the unregistered x86-64 and Aarch64",
    consequence: "One label spans a GPU virtual ISA and at least three physical machine architectures. PTX is explicitly a virtual ISA translated at install time to a target-specific instruction set, so it is not even the same KIND of thing as the others. This is the machine_code coproduct's fusion showing up again in the prose layer; see machine_code_fusion_note for the type-layer sighting."
  }
]

fn legacy_frontier_label_finding_count() -> Int {
  fold(legacy_frontier_label_findings, init: 0, f: fn(acc, r) { acc + 1 })
}

fn unregistered_target_finding_count() -> Int {
  fold(unregistered_target_findings, init: 0, f: fn(acc, r) { acc + 1 })
}

data project_grounded_targets_note: String = "The two PROJECT-grounded targets, and they are here for a reason beyond being cheap: ProjectGrounding had zero inhabitants, so an entire arm of the grounding coproduct was unexercised. These are also the two rows that could not have existed under a universal external subject carrier — the .dag language and the controlled-English medium are this project's OWN targets, with no independently governed upstream to cite, and forcing them through an external scope would have made them claim a governance they do not have. Their authority declarations are real and symbolic: dag_item_forms is the .dag surface-form authority, FormalSentence the controlled-English one. Neither carries an extdeps_model_scope and neither should. They are also now PROJECTABLE, which they were not while the join key was an external subject reference — the neutral DeclarationRef key is what lets one projection roster span both grounding arms instead of silently serving only external targets."

data jsx_registry_role_note: String = "JSX'S REGISTRY ROLE IS AN OPEN DECISION, SURFACED RATHER THAN SETTLED BY REGISTRATION (cursor review 48122, P1). This registry registered JSX as a parked P3 target because a live surface is named extdeps.languages.react_jsx and the census must account for it. The reviewer's objection is fair: the web direction treats JSX as an OPTIONAL PROJECTION and React as a projection rather than a second component authority, so quietly adding JSX as an independently supported target enlarges the strategy's target universe by a side effect of census bookkeeping. THE QUESTION, stated so it can be answered rather than inherited: is JSX an independently supported target MEDIUM, or a syntax CAPABILITY of the web/ECMAScript path? WHAT IS NOT IN DOUBT is the subject: the upstream JSX specification states in its own text that JSX is independent of React and has no defined semantics, so citing it separately from React is correct either way and the carrier stands whichever role is chosen. WHAT THE DISPOSITION MEANS TODAY: TargetDecisionPending, not TargetParked. Parked would assert a settled strategy slot awaiting implementation; pending says the slot itself is undecided, which is the honest state. Registering it as pending keeps the surface accounted for — a census that dropped the row would hide a real target-bearing module — without asserting the answer."

data d0_scope_boundary_note: String = "WHAT THIS REGISTRY IS AND IS NOT, so it is not read as closing more than it does (cursor review 48122, P1 — a fair objection to how this work could be presented). This is the D0 IDENTITY AND GROUNDING slice: which targets exist, what upstream authority grounds each, and which surfaces join which subject. IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE SUPPORT REGISTRY. The support contract requires every registered target to carry declared support PROFILES, required CAPABILITY sets, EVIDENCE refs, and checker bindings in one typed registry, and none of those exist here. What is here in their place is deliberately weaker and should be read as such: priority appears only as free text inside parked reasons, and there are no capability or profile rows at all. Anyone treating a registration in this file as a support claim is reading it wrong — grounding says a target has an authority behind it, not that anything about it is supported."

data governing_contract_id_resolution_note: String = "WHY THIS ROW CARRIES A MODULE NAME AND NOT THE GOVERNING CONTRACT IDENTIFIERS, and the answer is a publication boundary rather than a citation gap. The strategy authority that governs Rust is not in this repository: it lives in a separate private overlay, so its contract identifiers cannot be resolved — or verified — from here, and a sweep of this tree finds nothing no matter how thorough. THAT IS A FIFTH FAILURE SHAPE, worth naming because it is NOT the hand-carried-string class this lane spent the day fixing: those strings named nothing anywhere, while these name something real that is invisible from where the check stands. A measurement that cannot reach an authority must not conclude the authority is absent. WHY THE IDS ARE NOT SIMPLY COPIED IN: the private overlay runs an opt-in-public model in which publishing is an explicit act, so transcribing its identifiers into a public row would be a PUBLICATION DECISION taken by a registry edit — above this lane, and not repaired by the identifiers looking innocuous. The interim carried here is v2.compiler.self_host.emitter_producer_provenance, a real module in this repository that a reader can open, which is weaker than the governing contract but resolvable rather than dangling. It replaced v2.compiler.self_host.frontier when the self-host frontier roster was deleted; the first repoint went to emit_coverage_frontier and was wrong, because that module answers whether a witness body runs native (its axis a) and explicitly disclaims module self-emission (its axis b) — the name resembled the deleted one, which is not a reason. Rust is genuinely governed by MORE THAN ONE contract, which is why the carrier is plural and why this single entry is an interim rather than the truth. NEXT TRIGGER: the operator rules on whether the strategy keys are published; if they are, this field takes the exact governing identifiers and this note deletes. THE ECMASCRIPT AND C++ DECISION IDS ARE A DIFFERENT CASE: they name a document section in THIS repository, which is a positional citation — weaker than a symbol, but openable."


