data census_path_resolution_note: String = "EVERY module_path IN THIS CENSUS WAS VERIFIED AGAINST THE REAL MODULE TREE, after review 48122 caught one that named nothing. The reviewer found the SPICE row pointing at extdeps.formats.spice, which is not a module — the surface is v2.extdeps.formats.spice, as this registry's own SPICE subject carrier already said. Identifying that row made a phantom worse: an unidentified phantom is merely a surface nobody can find, while an IDENTIFIED one asserts a join that can never be exercised, and it hid the real SPICE surface's absence from the census behind an apparently-accounted-for row. SWEEPING THE WHOLE CENSUS RATHER THAN THE ONE CITED ROW FOUND TWO MORE, both older and both the same root cause: extdeps.rust.cargo and extdeps.python.interpreter are FILE PATHS transcribed as module paths (dag/extdeps/rust/cargo.dag and dag/extdeps/python/python.dag), but the modules those files declare are extdeps.cargo and extdeps.python. That is DESIGN section 3's 'paths are discriminators, not gospel' violated in the direction that is hardest to see — a module path that LOOKS derived from a real file. They are corrected to the real modules and, per surface_locus_grain_note, moved to MODULE grain rather than being given invented decl_names: 'cargo' and 'python_interpreter' are not declarations either, and inventing one would be a nickname for a module. THE NINE REMAINING NON-EXACT PATHS ARE LEGITIMATE AND WERE CHECKED INDIVIDUALLY: the nine extdeps.languages family paths (rust, typescript, python, go, c, css, json, yaml, lean) each name a FAMILY of two to six real modules, and v2.workflow.bash is a string prefix covering v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize and v2.workflow.bash_emit — the same prefix grain the containment lens uses. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH, and it is the honest limit: the sweep was done ONCE, by hand, at this revision. Nothing prevents the next phantom, because the census is hand-declared. The construction-side fix is the census being DERIVED from the module tree — already named as the residue-census obligation in language_target_registry_maximum_conclusion — at which point a path that names no module becomes unwritable rather than reviewable."

data runtime_checker_subject_note: String = "THE CHECKER ROWS BIND TO THE TARGET THEY REALIZE, AND THIS REVERSES THIS FILE'S EARLIER REASONING (cursor review 48122, P0; the lane owner, whose rows these are, agreed and asked that their original intent be overruled). An earlier revision left them unidentified on the argument that a toolchain is not a language. That argument is TRUE AND IRRELEVANT, and mistaking one for the other is the actual defect: LanguageTargetSurfaceRow.target answers WHICH TARGET THIS SURFACE REALIZES, not whether the tool IS the language. This registry already treats run_rust_smoke and run_python_smoke exactly that way — nobody claimed those smoke functions were the Rust and Python languages — so leaving cargo and the interpreter unidentified was inconsistent within one file. Bound: cargo realizes Rust, the Python interpreter realizes Python. Cargo and CPython keep their own separate upstream identities and would take their own cited subject declarations if either is ever registered in its own right; binding a surface to a target does not merge the two. Leaving them unidentified would have permanently conflated tool identity with associated target and stopped the unidentified population reaching zero for a reason that was never real."


data runtime_checker_rows: List<std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow> = [
  identified_module_row(authority: RuntimeChecker, module_path: "extdeps.cargo", subject: rust_language_subject_ref),
  identified_module_row(authority: RuntimeChecker, module_path: "extdeps.python", subject: python_language_subject_ref),
]

data legacy_frontier_row_note: String = "The legacy frontier surface stays unidentified because it is a MULTI-TARGET prose table, not a surface belonging to one subject: gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier carries a row per target for a dozen targets at once. It cannot join a single subject, and the join kernel is right to report it. Its dissolution is the migration of those prose rows onto per-subject carriers — now possible for the first time, because until this change there were no subjects to migrate them ONTO. That migration is not done here; what this change removes is the reason it was blocked. The projections below carry legacy_frontier_label so each prose row can be matched to its canonical subject by name rather than by eye when that work happens."

data legacy_frontier_rows: List<std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow> = [
  unidentified_module_row(authority: LegacyFrontier, module_path: "gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier"),
]

fn identified_decl_row(
  authority: std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String,
  decl_name: String,
  subject: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef
) -> std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: std.language_target_identity.SurfaceDeclaration {
      ref: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
        module_path: module_path,
        decl_name: decl_name,
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    target: Present { value: subject }
  }
}

fn identified_module_row(
  authority: std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String,
  subject: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef
) -> std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: std.language_target_identity.SurfaceModule { module_path: module_path },
    target: Present { value: subject }
  }
}

data bash_vocabulary_join_note: String = "The two Bash vocabulary rows below are identified, and their significance CHANGED when the other subjects landed (this note previously said Bash was 'the registry's only live join today' and that 'everything else is unidentified because Bash is the only subject that exists yet' — both true when written, both false the moment D0 registered 26 subjects, and left standing they made this module assert its own emptiness while listing a full registry; corrected after review 48113). What these rows demonstrate now is narrower and still worth stating: they are the join for a target whose surfaces are confined by MODULE PREFIX rather than by a target-model declaration, so they exercise the SurfaceModule locus grain against a real registered subject rather than a fixture. The interesting Bash fact today is not that it is the only join — it is the projection two rows down, where GNU Bash is a fully registered, cited subject with NO legacy language id at all, because std.languages carries rust, go, python and typescript rows and no bash row."

data target_vocabulary_rows: List<std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow> = [
  identified_module_row(authority: TargetVocabulary, module_path: "extdeps.languages.typescript.program", subject: typescript_language_subject_ref),
  identified_module_row(authority: TargetVocabulary, module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.bash", subject: gnu_bash_subject_ref),
  identified_module_row(authority: TargetVocabulary, module_path: "v2.workflow.bash", subject: gnu_bash_subject_ref),
]

data language_target_surface_census: List<std.language_target_identity.LanguageTargetSurfaceRow> = concat(
  concat(
    concat(std_language_catalogue_rows, emit_host_execution_rows),
    concat(target_model_declaration_rows, runtime_checker_rows)
  ),
  concat(legacy_frontier_rows, target_vocabulary_rows)
)

data language_target_projection_note: String = "Requirement D. Twenty-five projections — one per external subject, PLUS the .dag language, which only became projectable when the neutral DeclarationRef join key landed: under the old ExternalSubjectRef key the .dag language and the controlled-English medium would have had to be wrapped in a carrier documented as a reference to an independently governed upstream subject, which is exactly what it is not. The controlled-English medium is deliberately left UNPROJECTED: a registered subject with no projection is a real state the registry must report as Absent rather than fabricate a name for, and it is the live row that keeps that control exercised instead of hypothetical. Each projection is a set of ATTRIBUTES each a set of ATTRIBUTES hanging off the canonical subject rather than a second identity — the direction rule in language_target_projection_law is what makes them safe: projecting a subject to its LanguageId is total, while looking a LanguageId up REFUSES when no registered subject claims it. THE ABSENCES ARE THE FINDINGS AND THEY ARE LOAD-BEARING. legacy_language_id is Present on exactly four rows — rust, go, python, typescript — because std.languages contains exactly those four Language rows; every other registered subject, INCLUDING BASH, has no legacy id at all, which is why a LanguageId could never have served as the identity key for this registry. legacy_frontier_label is absent for html, css, json, yaml, commonmark and jsx because the prose self-host frontier table never carried rows for them: it is a table about compilation targets, and the structured media were outside its scope. Where a label IS carried it is the frontier's exact spelling, including the two rows whose spelling is itself a finding — C and C++ appear there as 'cpp — Phase-0-C' and 'cpp — full C target', two rows of one prose table for what are two DIFFERENT languages under one fused label, and PTX and RISC-V both map to the single label 'machine_code / ptx'. Those fused labels are precisely what per-subject carriers replace."

